+## Known limitations
+
+| Area | Limitation |
+|---|---|
+| **PA (preamp)** | CAT manual v1.01 documents `Set: PAP1P2;` (e.g. `PA10;` = on). This is incorrect -- the firmware silently ignores P2. The correct format is `PA1;` / `PA0;` (TS-480 format, no P2). The ANSWER format `PAP1P2;` is correct. |
+| **NL (noise blanker level)** | `NL000` is ignored by the firmware (FW 1.10): the level stays at its previous value (measured: `NL000` at level 005, read-back still `NL005`). `NL001` (1/16 ≈ 6 %) is the smallest effective value; the test script uses it as the lower bound. CAT manual 1.04 states 000 to 016. |
+| **VD (VOX delay)** | Under FW 1.10 values below 500 ms are also accepted and read back (measured: `VD0000`, `VD0100`). Whether the hardware applies them cannot be checked via CAT. The test script checks 500 ms (`val.i=5`, `VD0500`) and 1200 ms. |
+| **SD (break-in delay)** | Older firmware versions may cap the maximum value below 1200ms. |
+| **SM (S-meter)** | The Kenwood function for unknown models sends bare `SM;` instead of `SM0;`. A custom implementation is therefore required. |
+| **Passband / filter width (FLT key "NAR")** | The rig reports no filter width via CAT (`MD;` returns the mode only). The width shown by `rigctl m` is a nominal value assumed by Hamlib (CW 200 Hz, SSB 2700 Hz) or the last value set, held in the Hamlib cache -- not a hardware value. The FLT key (display "NAR", variable filter) can neither be set nor read via CAT. |
+| **TX (PTT)** | Bare `TX;` without P1 is rejected by the rig. P1 is mandatory. Only TX0 (mic/normal) and TX2 (tune) are documented in the CAT manual. `RIG_PTT_ON_DATA` is mapped to TX0 -- TX1 is not documented. Mapping to TX0 provides maximum compatibility with logging and digital mode software (WSJT-X, fldigi). |
+| **RIT/XIT** | RU/RD P1 is in 10Hz steps (values 00000-00500 = 0-5000Hz). Hamlib RIT value in Hz is divided by 10. |
+| **Menu item 33 (RTS as PTT)** | If menu item 33 is set to RTS=ON, the rig goes to transmit immediately on connect: Linux asserts RTS HIGH when opening the serial port, and the RGO ONE interprets this as PTT. **Menu item 33 must be set to OFF** when using CAT (rigctld). |
+| **MW — delete memory channel (delete_channel)** | `MW P1=0, FREQ=0` works from **FW 1.10** (verified on hardware, 2026-10-07); FW 1.09 rejected it with `?`. The backend reads every written or deleted slot back via `MR`. In memory mode the firmware ignores `MW` without answer; the backend then returns `-RIG_ERJCTED` (switch the rig to VFO mode with the M/V button first). Hamlib 4.7.1 itself never calls `delete_channel`; it is only reachable through the backend capabilities. |
+| **PTT via RTS on Windows** | The Windows CDC ACM driver `usbser.sys` only sends the USB control request `SET_CONTROL_LINE_STATE` to the device when DTR changes — pure RTS-only changes are silently discarded by the driver. The firmware therefore never receives the RTS state change. This driver bug has been known since 2008 and has never been fixed by Microsoft. **Workaround (implemented in the backend):** After every RTS change, DTR is written back once with its current value unchanged (`post_set_rts` callback). This forces a `SET_CONTROL_LINE_STATE` request carrying the updated RTS state. The workaround is compiled in only on Windows (`#if defined(WIN32)`); it is not active on Linux or macOS, where the CDC ACM driver forwards RTS changes correctly. Affects only PTT via RTS (`--ptt-type RTS`); CAT PTT (`TX0`/`RX`) works without restriction on all platforms. |
+
+## Test script
+
+The automated test script `test-dev/test_rgo_one.py` starts rigctld automatically
+(if not already running), tests all CAT commands with set-and-verify semantics,
+and prints a summary.
+
+```bash
+cd rigs/rgo/test-dev
+
+# Standard test (no PTT, no tuner)
+python3 test_rgo_one.py -r /dev/ttyACM0 -s 9600
+
+# With shorter delay (faster, slightly less stable)
+python3 test_rgo_one.py -r /dev/ttyACM0 -s 9600 --delay 0.3
+
+# Use a running rigctld instance
+python3 test_rgo_one.py --port 4532
+
+# Include PTT test (transmits! connect antenna or dummy load)
+python3 test_rgo_one.py -r /dev/ttyACM0 -s 9600 --ptt
+
+# Include antenna tuner cycle (transmits! TX2 = full tune cycle)
+python3 test_rgo_one.py -r /dev/ttyACM0 -s 9600 --tune
+
+# Keep rigctld running after test
+python3 test_rgo_one.py -r /dev/ttyACM0 -s 9600 --no-stop
+```
+
+Options:
+
+| Option | Description | Default |
+|---|---|---|
+| `-r DEVICE` | Serial device | `/dev/ttyACM0` |
+| `-s BAUD` | Baud rate | `9600` |
+| `--port PORT` | rigctld port (use running instance) | `4532` |
+| `--delay SEC` | Pause between set and get | `0.5` |
+| `--ptt` | Include PTT test (transmits!) | off |
+| `--tune` | Include antenna tuner cycle (transmits!) | off |
+| `--no-stop` | Keep rigctld running after test | off |
+
+Expected result (with `--ptt` and `--tune`):
+
+```
+--- Raw CAT commands (not in Hamlib API): AI, FS, PB ---
+  [OK]   AI: off (disabled by Hamlib on connect)  (AI0;)
+         (waiting ~1.5 s for AI2 periodic IF frame ...)
+  [OK]   AI2: periodic IF frame received and parsed  (freq=7.0260 MHz  mode=CW  tx=0)
+  [OK]   AI2: restored AI0  (AI0;)
+  [OK]   AI1: change-notification mode set  (AI1;)
+  [OK]   AI1: IF frame received after BU (band up)  (freq=10.1150 MHz  mode=CW)
+  [OK]   AI1: IF frame received after BD (band restored)  (freq=7.0260 MHz)
+  [OK]   AI: final verify AI0 (auto-info disabled)  (AI0;)
+  [OK]   FS: query fine tuning  (FS1;)
+  [OK]   FS: set off (FS0)  (FS0;)
+  [OK]   FS: restore (FS1)  (FS1;)
+  [OK]   PB: query memory playback  (ch=0 buf=00 (stop))
+========================================
+RESULT: 92/93 passed
+  OK:      92
+  FAILED:  0
+  SKIPPED: 1
+========================================
+```
+
+Without `--ptt` and `--tune`, PTT and tuner cycle are skipped
+(approx. 85/93 passed, approx. 8 skipped).
+The one permanent SKIP (`get_channel via Hamlib API`) is an upstream limitation:
+the netrigctl backend does not forward channel ops over the network; it works
+directly (`rigctl -m 43001 h 0 1`).
+The AI1 BD restore test may also be skipped if the rig is already on the lowest band
+(no band-change event is triggered by BD).
